---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Spousal Support Guides | spousalsupportcalc.com';
const description = 'Plain-English guides on spousal support calculation, state guidelines, dispute strategy, and common misconceptions.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Spousal Support Guides</h1>
  <p>Reviewed by the <a href="/editorial">editorial team</a>. These guides go beyond the calculator to cover how spousal support actually gets decided &mdash; the formulas courts reference, the ways state rules diverge, the misconceptions that trip up both paying and receiving spouses, and the practical steps that matter once support is contested. Read the <a href="/methodology">methodology</a> for the math behind the calculator itself, or start here for the context around it.</p>

  <h2>Understanding support</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guides/how-spousal-support-is-calculated">How spousal support is calculated</a> &mdash; The two paths a calculation can take depending on state and case stage: formula-based temporary support versus discretionary permanent support, and why the same income numbers can produce very different results under each.</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/state-spousal-support-guidelines">State spousal support guidelines</a> &mdash; A state-by-state look at how support law diverges, from California's Santa Clara formula to Texas's hard duration cap, and why the state you're filing in can matter as much as your income.</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/common-spousal-support-misconceptions">Common spousal support misconceptions</a> &mdash; The mistaken beliefs that both paying and receiving spouses bring into a case, and what the law actually says instead.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Practical action</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guides/what-to-do-in-spousal-support-dispute">What to do in a spousal support dispute</a> &mdash; The decisions in the first few months of a contested divorce that shape a support outcome for years, laid out as a practical sequence rather than legal theory.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>None of these guides substitute for advice from a family-law attorney licensed in your state. Support law is one of the most locally variable areas of family law, and a guide written for general audiences can't account for your judge, your county's local rules, or the specific facts of your marriage &mdash; use the <a href="/">calculator</a> and these guides to understand the landscape, then bring the specifics to a lawyer.</p>
</BaseLayout>
